264:Every comparison arm receives **identical online lessons** to the diary arm (same inputs, same pulses, same order). The five replay controls (no old replays, filler, moved timing, no pulse, event-shuffled) are copies of the diary arm's replay log that change **only the replays of old lessons**, meaning entries written in an earlier block. Replays of the current block's own lessons stay exactly as in the diary arm. So through the first block every replay control is in exactly the diary arm's state, and after that the only difference is what happened to the old-lesson replays, which is what the main hypothesis is about. No state is copied between arms after the first block, so later differences are the accumulated effect of each arm's own replay changes, not an isolated intervention from identical starts. The dry run checks that the first-block snapshot of each replay control equals the diary arm's, bit for bit. Stage 2 does this with a fixed tape. In stage 1 the diary arm makes its own choices, as in the original task, and each added arm is **yoked** to them: it receives the diary arm's chosen input and pulse at every trial instead of choosing. The task's four original arms keep their own choices and serve only to reproduce the original marks. The case-recall arms and the two learners are run in stage 2 as descriptive results and are decisive only in stage 3.
269:| **No diary** | Online lessons only. Its first-block learning also lacks the diary's replays, so diary versus no diary mixes better learning with better keeping; the replay controls separate the two | Baseline; the Volume 2 arm |
282:**Case recall, exact rule.** Keys, all fixed before data with the `features` stream: (a) raw input vector; (b) Kenyon-cell rates: the rates of the untrained learner in one forward pass on the input, with no learning and no dopamine; (c) random features: a projection z = Wx, where W has one row per Kenyon cell with independent standard normal entries drawn from the `features` stream, row by row. Keep the k units with the largest z (ties go to the lower unit index); the kept units carry max(z, 0) and all others are zero. Store contents, for the case-recall arms below: the final-diary lookup reads the evaluated fly's frozen final diary; the full-history lookup has one entry per original lesson receipt, and replays are never stored as cases. k is the mean number of active Kenyon cells per development input at the untrained state, rounded half up, computed once from the development inputs before any data. Each key is scaled to unit length; distance is Euclidean. A candidate's score is the inverse-distance-weighted mean of the pulse signs (+1 reward, −1 punishment) of its 3 nearest stored lessons, weight 1 / (d + 10⁻⁹); if any stored lesson is at distance below 10⁻¹², the score is the mean sign over those exact matches only. Ties and tie tolerance follow the fly's scoring. The bar is the best of the three keys in each version, chosen per stage on the final result: this favours the control.
284:**Simple learners, exact rule.** Score = w · φ(x), with w starting at zero. In stage 3 the learner applies every event of the matched tape, original lessons and replays alike, as an update. On each lesson or replay with pulse sign s: w ← w + s φ(x) / (‖φ(x)‖² + 10⁻⁹). φ is the raw input (the dense vector of Eyes v2 current on each photoreceptor, zero where the stimulus is absent) or the random features above. There is no learning rate to tune: because w starts at zero and every update would be multiplied by the same rate, a rate only rescales all scores and cannot change any ranking.
298:5. **Not extra practice:** in every seed, the diary keeps at least as many items correct at each test after new learning as the five replay controls. In every seed where the no-old-replays arm showed damage after new learning (at the end of learning or after the reversal), the diary also beats each of the five replay controls at that test: more items correct, or, with equal counts, a higher median margin ratio. A seed where the no-old-replays arm showed no such damage cannot show a benefit and is not required to. If in a seed the filler arm keeps fewer items than the no-old-replays arm, filler damaged learning there, and the filler comparison is reported as uninformative for that seed.
332:7. **Beats the practice controls:** more A pairs correct after B than no diary, no old replays, filler, moved timing, no pulse and event-shuffled, each with bootstrap lower limit above 0. If the filler arm ends B with fewer A pairs than the no-old-replays arm (lower limit above 0), filler damaged learning; that comparison is then reported as uninformative and the others decide. Because the replay controls start B in exactly the diary arm's state, those five comparisons measure keeping, not learning.
349:**Matched control input.** The matched tape has four fields only: the delivered stimulus, the external teaching input (or none), the event kind (lesson or replay) and the order. Replay logs (dopamine factors, update sizes, the chosen entry) are fly-internal and are not on the tape. Each control arm with a diary receives, for the fly it is compared with, the tape up to the exact receipt cutoff of that fly's selected snapshot, including whether that snapshot's last replay was applied; no event after the cutoff is given. Each control then uses the tape as follows: the final-diary lookup reads that fly's frozen diary at the cutoff; the full-history lookup has one entry per original lesson receipt (replays are never stored as cases); the simple learners apply every tape event as an update. The protocol's missing-idea and filler conditions are kept as the protocol defines them, so a control that lacks an idea never receives the omitted lessons. Fly arms choose for themselves, so they see different lessons; for the central attribution test, the learner arms are matched to the evaluated full fly's tape. The auditor checks byte equality of the matched tape for each comparison, separately from each arm's own update records.
358:Its wording is then limited to: the fly with a disclosed replay diary combined three separately taught chess ideas on unfamiliar problems better than the listed case-recall and simple-learner controls. A case classifier not among those controls is not ruled out. Failing any of 1 to 4 leaves reasoning unsupported, whatever stages 1 and 2 showed.
